::GEO_U04_L03_GP_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
Two triangles have two pairs of congruent corresponding angles. Which criterion justifies similarity?
{
=A. AA similarity#AA similarity applies because two angle pairs determine the third angle pair, so the triangles have the same shape.
~B. SSS congruence#Congruence requires equal side lengths, but the evidence given is angle equality for similarity.
~C. HL congruence#HL applies only to right-triangle congruence, not a two-angle similarity argument.
~D. CPCTC#CPCTC is used after congruence is proven, not to justify triangle similarity.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_GP_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
The sides of one triangle are 4, 6, 8. The corresponding sides of another are 6, 9, 12. What similarity criterion is supported?
{
~A. ASA congruence#ASA proves congruence when side and angle measures match exactly, not proportional side lengths.
=B. SSS similarity#The ratios 6/4, 9/6, and 12/8 are all 1.5, so all side pairs are proportional.
~C. SAS congruence#SAS congruence needs equal included sides, but these sides are proportional, not equal.
~D. No similarity conclusion#A similarity conclusion is supported because all corresponding side ratios are equal.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_GP_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
Two triangles have one congruent included angle and the adjacent side ratios are both 2. Which criterion justifies similarity?
{
~A. AA similarity#AA similarity requires two angle pairs; this evidence includes one angle and two side ratios.
~B. SSS similarity#SSS similarity requires all three side ratios, but only two adjacent side ratios are given.
=C. SAS similarity#SAS similarity uses a congruent included angle and proportional adjacent side lengths.
~D. Reflection symmetry#Reflection symmetry maps a figure onto itself and does not by itself justify triangle similarity here.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_GP_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
A dilation maps triangle ABC to a triangle congruent to triangle DEF. What conclusion is justified?
{
~A. triangle ABC is not related to triangle DEF#The transformation sequence gives evidence connecting the triangles.
~B. Only one side is proportional#A dilation scales all lengths by the same factor, not just one side.
~C. The triangles must be identical in size#A dilation can change size, so similarity is supported even when congruence is not.
=D. triangle ABC ∼ triangle DEF#A dilation followed by congruence-preserving motion supports similarity between the original and final triangles.
}

::GEO_U04_L03_GP_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L03_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.3
Question:
Which statement is the strongest similarity justification?
{
=A. Two angle pairs are congruent, so the triangles are similar by AA.#This names the evidence and the correct similarity criterion.
~B. The triangles look like the same shape.#Appearance is not enough; a mathematical criterion must be named.
~C. One pair of sides is proportional.#One side ratio alone is not enough to prove triangle similarity.
~D. The triangles are in the same diagram.#Location in a diagram does not justify similarity.
}


